<interrogationes unitas="rivus_terminalis">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I turn raw terminal input bytes into key, mouse and paste events?
    <responsum symbolum="rivus_creare rivus_eventum rivus_mora_ms rivus_spatium rivus_tradere rivus_moram" via="tessera/fontes/tessera_eventum.c">The
      library never reads or keeps a clock; you drive it. Loop: drain
      events with rivus_eventum until it returns FALSUM; then ask
      rivus_mora_ms how long to wait (0 = nothing pending, use your own
      timeout) and read at most rivus_spatium bytes with that timeout;
      if bytes came, give them to rivus_tradere and loop; if the wait
      ran out while something was pending (or rivus_spatium was 0), call
      rivus_moram and loop. The tempus argument of rivus_eventum and
      rivus_moram is only stamped onto the events (tessera passes 0).
      tessera/fontes/tessera_eventum.c is the reference loop.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why must I call rivus_moram, and what does it do to a lone ESC?
    <responsum symbolum="rivus_moram rivus_mora_ms">Bytes
      alone cannot tell the Escape key from the start of an escape
      sequence, so a pending ESC (or half a sequence, half a UTF-8 rune,
      an unfinished paste) waits for silence. rivus_mora_ms says how
      long: RIVUS_MORA_FUGAE_MS (25 ms) normally, RIVUS_MORA_GLUTINI_MS
      (3 s) inside a bracketed paste. When that silence has passed,
      rivus_moram resolves it: ESC becomes the Escape key, ESC ESC two of
      them, ESC x becomes Alt+x, other partial sequences (and a half X10
      mouse report) are dropped, half a rune is dropped, and an
      unterminated paste is finished and marked truncated. A half SGR
      mouse report is kept in case the rest arrives, and a lone ESC,
      although already reported as Escape, is also kept so that a mouse
      report split right after it still decodes.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I keep a pointer to an event's text after the next call?
    <responsum symbolum="rivus_eventum rivus_eventum_coalitum eventus_cauda_lectio_incipit">No.
      Event text (and coalesced mouse samples) live in the event
      queue's payload buffers, which are emptied when the queue is
      empty and decoding starts again (eventus_cauda_lectio_incipit).
      Views are valid until the next rivus_eventum (or
      rivus_eventum_coalitum); copy anything you need to keep.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I turn on mouse reporting, bracketed paste, focus events or kitty keys?
    <responsum symbolum="rivus_modos_intrare rivus_modos_exire rivus_modos_exeundi">Call
      rivus_modos_intrare with RIVUS_MODUS_* bits and a buffer of at
      least RIVUS_MODI_MAXIMUM bytes; it returns the bytes YOU must
      write to the terminal (the library is pure and writes nothing).
      SUPER implies MUS, DEPOSITIO implies GLUTINUM. It returns 0 and
      changes nothing if modes are already entered, the buffer is too
      small, or no known bit is set (a caller cannot tell these apart
      from the return value). Entering also pushes an
      EVENTUS_FACULTATES event. On exit, rivus_modos_exire gives the
      reverse sequence (kitty popped first) and clears the state;
      rivus_modos_exeundi gives the same bytes without changing state,
      so a signal handler can keep them ready.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what is the difference between rivus_eventum and rivus_eventum_coalitum?
    <responsum symbolum="rivus_eventum rivus_eventum_coalitum">rivus_eventum
      decodes lazily, one lexeme at a time until an event exists, and
      never merges mouse motion: a consumer that must see every report
      uses it. rivus_eventum_coalitum, when the queue is empty, decodes
      ALL bytes already given (up to half the queue), so consecutive
      motion merges into one event with the samples attached, the way
      the window does it. Ordinary Eventus consumers want the
      coalesced form.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can a paste of a file path from Finder arrive as a file drop instead of text?
    <responsum symbolum="rivus_modos_intrare interpres_depositio">Yes,
      if RIVUS_MODUS_DEPOSITIO is entered: a whole, untruncated paste
      that parses as absolute paths (shell quoting with backslash,
      single and double quotes, or file:// URIs with %XX escapes) is
      promoted to EVENTUS_DEPOSITIO. Anything else stays an ordinary
      paste text event. The drop position is the last mouse position
      seen BEFORE the drag, (0,0) if none, because the terminal cannot
      see a drag from another application; target by focus, not by
      position.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I hand rivus_tradere a large read buffer?
    <responsum symbolum="rivus_tradere rivus_spatium rivus_pendentes rivus_creare">It
      takes at most rivus_spatium bytes (the internal buffer is
      RIVUS_BUFFER, 256) and returns how many it accepted; the rest is
      yours to keep and offer again after draining events, so read no
      more than rivus_spatium in the first place. A paste body is
      collected separately into a 64 KB buffer and is flagged truncated
      beyond that. rivus_creare takes those two 64 KB buffers and the
      event queue from the piscina you pass. rivus_pendentes reports
      bytes not yet consumed, to check that a stream ended
      cleanly.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
